    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "definition",
      "value": "The indigenous religions of the Han Chinese, or, by metonymy, of all the populations of the Chinese cultural sphere. It includes the syncretism of Confucianism, Taoism, and Buddhism, Wuism, as well as many new religious movements such as Chen Tao, Falun Gong, and Yiguandao.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0026",
        "chunk_title": "Folk religions + Indian religions",
        "chunk_order": 25,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "examples",
      "value": "Other folk and new religions of East Asia and Southeast Asia such as Korean shamanism, Chondogyo, and Jeung San Do in Korea; indigenous Philippine folk religions in the Philippines; Shinto, Shugendo, Ryukyuan religion, and Japanese new religions in Japan; Satsana Phi in Laos; Vietnamese folk religion, and Cao Đài, Hòa Hảo in Vietnam.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0026",
        "chunk_title": "Folk religions + Indian religions",
        "chunk_order": 25,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "definition",
      "value": "Indian religions are practiced or were founded in the Indian subcontinent. They are sometimes classified as the dharmic religions, as they all feature dharma, the specific law of reality and duties expected according to the religion.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0026",
        "chunk_title": "Folk religions + Indian religions",
        "chunk_order": 25,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
